liblink
-------

liblink contains the runtime object linker used by GHC. The Cabal package is
named liblink; the native library is named link. Native clients link with
-llink (liblink.a or the platform's shared-library equivalent).

The current extraction preserves the RTS linker interface through forwarding
functions in rts/LinkerCompat.c. The headers under include/liblink provide the
compatibility and RTS friend interfaces; they are internal interfaces, not a
stable public embedding API. Unload checking and the GC remain in the RTS.

The installed liblink.h declares the handle API. Its current entry points are
unsupported stubs: ll_new returns NULL, and functions returning ll_status
return LL_E_UNSUPPORTED. RTS clients continue to use the compatibility
interface.

Locking
-------

On native platforms, linker locks use pthread mutexes or Windows SRW locks in
both threaded and non-threaded RTS builds. Assertion-enabled pthread builds
use error-checking mutexes. Initialization and shutdown require exclusive
access; the mutex helpers do not implement one-time initialization.

Wasm deliberately preserves the existing single-threaded RTS behavior:
initialization, shutdown, lock, unlock, and held-lock assertions are no-ops
when wasm32_HOST_ARCH is defined. This is an explicit exception to the native
always-on locking rule. It provides no synchronization and does not establish
support for concurrent Wasm linker calls. Supporting those calls requires a
Wasm mutex backend and validation of that backend.

The Wasm helper branch has focused compile and no-op checks. These checks do
not qualify a complete GHC/WASI build or runtime.

Memory protection failures
--------------------------

Final OS memory-protection failures report LL_E_IO. Allocation and
address-space reservation failures report LL_E_NOMEM. If final protection
fails, the compatibility linker permanently quarantines that object
instance and its transitive dependents. The first operation preserves its
original diagnostic; later lookup, relocation, and initialization attempts
for affected objects fail with LL_E_STATE. Optimistic linking cannot turn
this failure into a placeholder address. A failed M32 flush also prevents
further allocation or flushing through that allocator.

Global resolution fails while a quarantined object remains loaded. Lookup
of an already-resolved independent object remains available. Pending
initializers skip quarantined objects and preserve the order and host hooks
of healthy objects. Ordinary unresolved-symbol retries keep their existing
behavior. The reported object state remains its last normal lifecycle phase;
the quarantine marker is private and introduces no new enum value.

Unload all affected objects before reloading them. Their mappings and
dependency identities remain owned until normal unload and GC disposal,
including objects whose recursive symbol lookup failed before registration.
A failed load can therefore leave a quarantined object registered for
unloading. Freshly loaded instances start without the marker. Previously
returned raw pointers cannot be revoked by the linker.

Initializer failures
--------------------

A null .init_array entry returns LL_E_INIT and releases the acquired program
environment exactly once. A failed initializer backend quarantines the object
and its transitive dependents under the policy above. The lookup that triggered
initialization returns NULL. Earlier constructor side effects remain; repeated
lookup or resolve cannot replay the executed prefix. Unload all affected objects
before reloading them. Pending initializer and finalizer metadata stays owned
until disposal, and finalizers run only after successful initialization.

Native load failure cleanup
----------------------------

After a final failed dlopen, the POSIX compatibility loader completes its load
hooks and copies the error before invoking the optional on_unload callback.
It then frees unregistered object metadata. The callback sees OBJECT_READY with
a NULL native handle. The caller owns the copied error. Metadata cleanup also
works when on_unload is absent.

Windows M32 reservation ownership
---------------------------------

Windows M32 mappings keep reservation records outside page payloads. Pool
transfers preserve page ownership. Discarding pages uses MEM_DECOMMIT on
the page range, and releases their logical ownership even if decommit
fails. Only an ownerless reservation can use MEM_RELEASE, with its original
base and a zero size. A failed base release keeps its record for retry.
Allocation-granularity padding is recorded separately from usable pages.

Shutdown drains the free-page pool and releases eligible reservations. It
preserves reservations still owned by live object allocators; the existing
compatibility shutdown does not destroy those allocators. Their disposal
must precede terminal reservation cleanup. Cleanup preserves an existing
primary error, and a failed attempt to make a page writable cannot write a
free-list header into that page. Page payload offsets, the pool limit,
32-page batching, and address placement policy remain unchanged.

Native Windows execution in CI is required to validate these OS paths.
Darwin source compilation does not qualify their Windows behavior.
